  <section id="what-is-dst" class="content-section reveal" aria-label="What is Daylight Saving Time">
    <h2>1. What is Daylight Saving Time?</h2>
    <p>
      Daylight Saving Time (DST) is a seasonal timekeeping adjustment where civil clocks are advanced by one hour during the summer months and set back to standard time in the autumn. The primary rationale is to align active human waking hours with natural daylight, effectively extending evening light at the expense of morning light.
    </p>
    <p>
      For example, during standard time, the sun might rise at 5:00 AM and set at 8:00 PM. By shifting the clock forward one hour under DST, the sun rises at 6:00 AM and sets at 9:00 PM. Because few people are active at 5:00 AM, moving that hour of daylight to the evening gives the public more usable outdoor light after work or school.
    </p>
    <p>
      However, DST does not create new daylight; it simply shifts when the daylight occurs relative to civil clocks. The total amount of daylight remains determined by the Earth's axial tilt and orbital position.
    </p>

