       ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->
  <section id="history-of-dst" class="content-section reveal" aria-label="History of DST">
    <h2>2. The History and Origin of DST</h2>
    <p>
      The history of Daylight Saving Time is a fascinating journey of scientific proposals, wartime survival strategies, and social debates:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>Benjamin Franklin (1784)</strong>: While serving as an American envoy in Paris, Franklin wrote a satirical essay suggesting that Parisians could save money on candles by waking up earlier to utilize morning sunlight. He proposed firing cannons at sunrise to wake the public, but he did not suggest changing civil clocks.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>George Hudson (1895)</strong>: An astronomer and entomologist in New Zealand, Hudson presented a paper to the Wellington Philosophical Society proposing a two-hour seasonal shift. He wanted more daylight hours after work to collect and study insects.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>William Willett (1907)</strong>: A British builder and avid golfer, Willett published a pamphlet titled "The Waste of Daylight," proposing that clocks be advanced by 20 minutes on four consecutive Sundays in April and reversed in September. He spent a significant portion of his personal fortune lobbying the British Parliament to introduce a Daylight Saving Bill. Despite backing from high-profile figures like Winston Churchill and Arthur Conan Doyle, the agricultural lobby and traditionalists blocked the bill repeatedly, fearing it would disrupt crop harvesting and train schedules. Willett unfortunately passed away in 1915, just one year before his proposal was officially adopted.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>World War I Adoption (1916)</strong>: As the war dragged on, Europe faced severe coal shortages. Seeking to conserve fuel for manufacturing ammunition and keeping homes warm, Germany and its ally Austria-Hungary officially implemented Daylight Saving Time on April 30, 1916. The United Kingdom, recognizing the energy conservation benefits, enacted the Summer Time Act of 1916 just three weeks later. The United States officially adopted DST in 1918 under the Standard Time Act, which also established the federal timezone system.
      </li>
    </ul>

