  <!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
       SECTION 3: HOW DST WORKS
       ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->
  <section id="how-dst-works" class="content-section reveal" aria-label="How DST Works">
    <h2>3. How DST Works: The Mechanics</h2>
    <p>
      The mechanics of DST are based on two annual clock transitions:
    </p>
    <article class="method-card" style="margin-bottom: 1.5rem;">
      <h3>Spring Transition (Spring Forward)</h3>
      <p>
        During the spring transition, clocks are advanced by one hour. The shift occurs at 2:00 AM local time, meaning the clock immediately skips to 3:00 AM. This results in:
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li>A 23-hour day.</li>
        <li>One hour of sleep lost on the transition night.</li>
        <li>Lighter evenings and darker mornings.</li>
      </ul>
    </article>

    <article class="method-card" style="margin-bottom: 1.5rem;">
      <h3>Autumn Transition (Fall Back)</h3>
      <p>
        During the autumn transition, clocks are set back by one hour. The shift occurs at 2:00 AM local time, meaning the clock immediately falls back to 1:00 AM, repeating that hour. This results in:
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li>A 25-hour day.</li>
        <li>One hour of sleep gained on the transition night.</li>
        <li>Lighter mornings and darker evenings.</li>
      </ul>
    </article>
    <p>
      The choice of 2:00 AM for the transition is intentional. It represents a time when most people are asleep, businesses are closed, and public transportation schedules are at their lowest density, minimizing daily disruption.
    </p>
  </section>

  <!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
       SECTION 4: GLOBAL ADOPTION
       ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->
  <section id="global-adoption" class="content-section reveal" aria-label="Global Adoption">
    <h2>4. DST Around the World: Global Adoption</h2>
    <p>
      DST is not observed universally. Its adoption is heavily influenced by geography and local political decisions:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>Northern Hemisphere</strong>: Observed in the United States, Canada, Europe, and parts of the Middle East. The transition months are March and November (or October in Europe).
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Southern Hemisphere</strong>: Because seasons are reversed, southern hemisphere regions (such as parts of Australia, New Zealand, Paraguay, and Chile) start DST in October and return to standard time in April.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Equatorial Regions</strong>: Tropical countries near the equator do not observe DST. Their daylight hours vary by less than an hour throughout the year, rendering clock shifts useless.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Countries Rejecting DST</strong>: Many major nations have abandoned DST. For example, China abandoned DST in 1992 to simplify national scheduling across its single massive timezone. Russia shifted to permanent standard time in 2014, and Turkey made daylight saving time permanent year-round in 2016.
      </li>
    </ul>

