       ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->
  <section id="north-america-dst" class="content-section reveal" aria-label="Daylight Saving in North America">
    <h2>5. Daylight Saving Time in North America</h2>
    <p>
      In the United States and Canada, DST rules are regulated by federal laws but executed at the state or provincial level:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>US Energy Policy Act of 2005</strong>: This act extended the duration of DST in the United States, starting it on the second Sunday of March and ending it on the first Sunday of November. This added four weeks of daylight saving time compared to the previous system.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Arizona and Hawaii Exceptions</strong>: Arizona does not observe DST because of its extreme summer heat. Staying on standard time keeps sunset earlier, reducing home air-conditioning costs in the evening. The Navajo Nation (which occupies parts of Arizona) does observe DST, but the Hopi Reservation (which lies entirely inside the Navajo Nation) does not. Hawaii does not observe DST due to its low latitude, where sunset times vary minimally.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Sunshine Protection Act</strong>: This proposed US federal bill aims to make Eastern Daylight Time permanent year-round. While the bill passed the Senate in 2022, it stalled in the House of Representatives due to debates over whether permanent standard time (EST) or permanent daylight time (EDT) is healthier for public sleep cycles.
      </li>
    </ul>

