  <!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
       SECTION 7: TECHNICAL IMPLEMENTATION
       ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->
  <section id="technical-implementation" class="content-section reveal" aria-label="Technical Implementation">
    <h2>7. Technical Implementation of DST</h2>
    <p>
      Managing daylight saving transitions is one of the most complex challenges in software engineering. Databases and operating systems rely on specific architectures to prevent temporal errors:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>Coordinated Universal Time (UTC)</strong>: Developers store all database timestamps in UTC. UTC is an atomic clock standard that does not observe DST or shift offsets. By using UTC as the database baseline, systems avoid double-entry conflicts.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>IANA Time Zone Database (tzdata)</strong>: Operating systems determine timezone offsets using the IANA Time Zone Database. This library documents historical and future DST changes for named locations (e.g., `America/New_York` or `Europe/London`).
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Javascript Date Parsing</strong>:
        <p>
          Developers use native client features to calculate localized daylight saving times without manual math. Review this typical code block:
        </p>
        <pre style="background: var(--panel); padding: 1rem; border-radius: 6px; border: 1px solid var(--line); overflow-x: auto; color: var(--text); font-family: monospace;">{`// Check if a date is in Daylight Saving Time in New York
function isNewYorkDST(date) {
  const jan = new Date(date.getFullYear(), 0, 1).getTimezoneOffset();
  const jul = new Date(date.getFullYear(), 6, 1).getTimezoneOffset();
  return date.getTimezoneOffset() < Math.max(jan, jul);
}
console.log("Is current date in DST?", isNewYorkDST(new Date()));`}</pre>
      </li>
    </ul>

