  <section id="common-mistakes" class="content-section reveal" aria-label="Common Mistakes">
    <h2>9. Common Mistakes &amp; Confusions</h2>
    <article class="method-card" style="margin-bottom: 1.5rem;">
      <h3>Mistake 1: Confusing Spring and Autumn Shifts</h3>
      <p>
        <strong>Fact:</strong> A common phrase to memorize clock transitions is **"Spring forward, Fall back."** Setting clocks backward in the spring would result in daylight being misaligned, leaving the evening dark and sunrise occurring too early.
      </p>
    </article>

    <article class="method-card" style="margin-bottom: 1.5rem;">
      <h3>Mistake 2: Writing EST in July</h3>
      <p>
        <strong>Fact:</strong> Because the East Coast observes EDT in the summer, writing "EST" on meeting invitations during July is technically incorrect. Calendars using automated parsing will schedule the event one hour late for international participants.
      </p>
    </article>

    <article class="method-card">
      <h3>Mistake 3: Assuming All States Change Clocks Simultaneously</h3>
      <p>
        <strong>Fact:</strong> Because Arizona and Hawaii do not observe DST, scheduling calls with colleagues in Phoenix during summer must account for the fact that Phoenix operates on Mountain Standard Time (MST, equivalent to PDT) during this season.
      </p>
    </article>
  </section>

